        "geology": "The Highland Boundary Fault divides the Research Station into two distinct geological zones. North of the fault are drifts derived from schistose rock.  To the south of the fault are drifts derived from Old Red Sandstone. ",
        "hydrology": "The area is drained by a number of  small headwater streams.  The ground water table is relatively high and there are many springs across higher ground. There is also a small loch. Some streams are virtually  emphemeral and run through deep gravels in the summer.",
        "soils": "The soils reflect the underlying geology which is influenced by The Highland Boundary Fault. North of the fault the soils are of the Strichen Association.  Brown forest soils and podzols of the lower slopes give way to peaty podzols and, on the highest ground, to peat. To the south of the fault the soils are humus-iron podzols on the lower slopes and peaty podzols  at higher elevations.",
        "vegetation": "The research station comprises 865 hectares (ha) of semi-natural plant communities, 60 ha of predominantly rotational grassland and 88 ha of permanent pasture. On the alluvial soils of the valley bottom the semi-natural vegetation is dominated by species-rich Agrostis-Festuca grassland. This gives way to species-poor Agrostis-Festuca on the lower slopes, with bracken (Pteridium aquilinum) also present. The higher slopes are dry heather (Calluna vulgaris) moor, with blaeberry (Vaccinium myrtillus), wavy hair grass (Deschampsia flexuosa) and bell heather (Erica cinerea) locally important. On the deeper peats at the highest altitude, cross-leaved heather (Erica tetralix) and cotton sedge (Eriophorum vaginatum) become co-dominant. The predominantly rotational grassland is reseeded with perennial ryegrass and white clover mixtures every 7-10 years. This provides grazing for the crossbred ewe flock, swards for experimental work, silage for winter feeding and aftermath grazing for finishing lambs."
      },
